<para>For problems 6 – 9, write each expression in factored form. Note that the leading coefficient for each expression is 1.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x^2 − 25x + 100\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the expression in factored form.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \((x − 20)(x − 5)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x^2 + 15x − 100\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the expression in factored form.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \((x + 20)(x − 5)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x^2− 15x − 100\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the expression in factored form.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((x − 20)(x + 5)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x^2 + 99x − 100\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the expression in factored form.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \((x + 100)(x − 1)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<title>Video: Analyzing factors of 100 and –100</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about analyzing the factors of 100 and –100 and applying them in factoring quadratic expressions.</para>
